---
import snapshot from '../data/index-companies.json';
const exampleCompanies = snapshot.companies.slice(0, 3);
const dollars = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', {
  style: 'currency',
  currency: 'USD',
});
---

<section
  class="learn-chapter buy-index-chapter"
  id="how-to-buy-spx"
  aria-labelledby="buy-index-title"
>
  <div class="buy-index-intro">
    <div>
      <span class="learn-eyebrow">02 / FROM INDEX TO INVESTMENT</span>
      <h2 id="buy-index-title">How to buy <em>SPX?</em></h2>
    </div>
    <p>
      You can’t buy the index itself. But you can own the investments behind it.
      Here’s where an ETF makes life simpler.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="buy-index-routes">
    <article class="index-diy" aria-labelledby="index-diy-title">
      <span class="learn-eyebrow">THE DO-IT-YOURSELF ROUTE</span>
      <h3 id="index-diy-title">Build the basket yourself.</h3>
      <p>
        You could buy all 500 companies and divide your money according to their
        index weights. Setting up 500 holdings is a lot of work—and keeping the
        basket accurate over time is harder still.
      </p>
      <figure class="diy-allocation-example">
        <figcaption>
          For example, with <strong>$1,000</strong> to invest…
        </figcaption>
        <div class="diy-company-amounts">
          {
            exampleCompanies.map((company) => (
              <div>
                <img
                  src={`/company-logos/${company.logo}`}
                  width="22"
                  height="22"
                  alt=""
                />
                <span>{company.name}</span>
                <strong>{dollars.format((1000 * company.weight) / 100)}</strong>
                <small>{company.weight.toFixed(2)}% weight</small>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
        <span class="diy-more-companies"
          >…and work out the amounts for the other 497 companies.</span
        >
        <small class="diy-data-note"
          >Illustration using the SPY holdings snapshot above, {
            snapshot.dateLabel
          }.</small
        >
      </figure>
      <ol class="diy-maintenance">
        <li>
          <span aria-hidden="true">01</span><p>
            <strong>Buy small pieces of shares.</strong> With a smaller budget, closely
            matching every weight generally requires fractional shares—buying part
            of a share. Your broker must support the stocks and amounts you need.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span aria-hidden="true">02</span><p>
            <strong>Keep up with changing weights.</strong> Tracking 500 holdings
            and matching their weights is difficult. Price changes move your holdings’
            weights naturally, but companies joining or leaving the index, share-count
            changes, dividends and new deposits still need your attention.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span aria-hidden="true">03</span><p>
            <strong>Watch the trading costs.</strong> If your broker charges per trade,
            hundreds of commissions could eat up all your profit. Some brokers offer
            $0 trades; spreads and other costs can still apply.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="diy-cost-example">
        <span>AN EXAMPLE AT $1 PER TRADE</span>
        <strong>500 purchases = $500 in commissions</strong>
        <span>Before you’ve even started maintaining the portfolio.</span>
      </div>
      <a
        class="learn-source"
        href="https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins/fractional-share-investing-buying-slice-instead-whole-share"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">Investor.gov · Fractional shares ↗</a
      >
    </article>

    <article class="index-etf-route" aria-labelledby="index-etf-route-title">
      <span class="learn-eyebrow">THE SIMPLER ROUTE</span>
      <h3 id="index-etf-route-title">Let an ETF do the work.</h3>
      <div class="etf-route-visual" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="etf-route-stocks">
          {
            snapshot.companies.slice(0, 5).map((company) => (
              <span>
                <img
                  src={`/company-logos/${company.logo}`}
                  width="21"
                  height="21"
                  alt=""
                />
              </span>
            ))
          }
          <span class="etf-route-more">+495</span>
        </div>
        <svg
          class="etf-route-connections"
          viewBox="0 0 300 38"
          preserveAspectRatio="none"
          ><path
            d="M25 0V14H150V38M75 0V14M125 0V14M175 0V14M225 0V14H150M275 0V14H225"
          ></path></svg
        >
        <div class="etf-route-token">
          <span class="learn-diamond">◆</span><strong>ETF</strong><span
            >ONE FUND.<br />THE WHOLE BASKET.</span
          >
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        With <strong>one S&amp;P 500 ETF in your portfolio</strong>, you have a
        stake in around <strong>500 companies through the fund</strong>. You buy
        the ETF through a broker; the fund handles the underlying stocks.
      </p>
      <ul class="etf-route-benefits">
        <li>
          <span aria-hidden="true">✓</span> One ETF holding, around 500 companies
        </li>
        <li>
          <span aria-hidden="true">✓</span> Portfolio weights managed for you
        </li>
        <li>
          <span aria-hidden="true">✓</span> Index membership changes handled by the
          fund
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="index-fees" aria-labelledby="index-fees-title">
        <span class="learn-eyebrow">WHAT ABOUT MANAGEMENT FEES?</span>
        <h4 id="index-fees-title">Doesn’t a fund take a big cut?</h4>
        <p>
          An index fund follows the index’s rules instead of paying managers to
          pick winning stocks. That generally costs less. The annual{' '}
          <strong>expense ratio</strong> includes management and other operating costs,
          and is deducted inside the fund.
        </p>
        <div class="fee-comparison" aria-label="Annual fund costs compared">
          <div class="fee-row fee-row-spy">
            <div>
              <span>SPY · S&amp;P 500 ETF</span><strong>0.0945%</strong>
            </div>
            <div class="fee-bar" aria-hidden="true">
              <span style="width:23.625%"></span>
            </div>
            <small>About <b>$9.45</b> a year per $10,000</small>
          </div>
          <div class="fee-row">
            <div>
              <span>U.S. stock mutual funds · average</span><strong
                >0.40%</strong
              >
            </div>
            <div class="fee-bar" aria-hidden="true">
              <span style="width:100%"></span>
            </div>
            <small>About <b>$40</b> a year per $10,000</small>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="fee-method">
          SPY fee checked October 11, 2026. Mutual fund average: 2025, weighted
          by assets, including active and index funds. Dollar examples assume a
          constant $10,000 balance. Trading commissions and spreads are
          separate. Index mutual funds can also be inexpensive.
        </p>
        <div class="fee-sources">
          <a
            class="learn-source"
            href="https://www.ssga.com/us/en/individual/etfs/state-street-spdr-sp-500-etf-trust-spy"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">State Street · SPY ↗</a
          >
          <a
            class="learn-source"
            href="https://www.iciglobal.org/news-release/mutual-fund-and-etf-fees-remained-near-historic-lows-in-2025"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">ICI · 2025 averages ↗</a
          >
        </div>
      </div>
      <a class="etf-route-next" href="#the-etf"
        >See how an ETF works <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a
      >
      <a
        class="learn-source"
        href="https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/mutual-funds-and-exchange-traded-4"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">Investor.gov · Index funds ↗</a
      >
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
